# Text of Proposition n:relabel (added 21 November 2026), applied by build_revision.py.
MAIN = r'''\begin{proposition}[Disclosure when labels can be gamed]\label{n:relabel}
Fix one supervisory cycle. A share $\chi\in(0,1)$ of feedback activity belongs to a class $H$ and the rest to a class $S$. Each retained unit of activity has net social value $n_H<0<n_S$, and $H$ traders gain $b_H>0$ per retained unit. Without disclosure the only restriction is a uniform retention $x\in[0,1]$ of all activity. With class tags the supervisor sets a retention $y$ for the $H$ label and $x\ge y$ for the $S$ label, and $H$ traders may route a share $z\in[0,1]$ of their activity through $S$ labels at resource cost $\gamma z^2/2$ per unit of $H$ activity, $\gamma>0$. An audit trail that links accounts to owners prevents relabeling. Write $a=-n_H$, $m=\chi n_H+(1-\chi)n_S$, $s=(1-\chi)n_S$ and $g=\gamma/\{b_H(2a+b_H)\}$.
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*),itemsep=1pt]
\item $H$ traders route $z^*=\min\{1,\,b_H(x-y)/\gamma\}$ through $S$ labels.
\item Without disclosure the welfare is $\max\{0,m\}$; with an audit trail it is $s$, before its cost. With class tags the optimal policy, and its gain $V_C$ over the uniform cut, are as follows. If $m\ge0$: $x=1$, the label gap is $x-y=\min\{1,ag\}$, and $V_C=\chi a^2g/2$ when $ag\le1$, $V_C=\chi a-\chi/(2g)$ otherwise. If $m<0$: $y=0$, $x=\min\{1,sg/\chi\}$, and $V_C=s^2g/(2\chi)$ when $sg\le\chi$, $V_C=s-\chi/(2g)$ otherwise. In every case the equilibrium hiding share lies strictly between $0$ and $1$.
\item $V_C$ is continuous and strictly increasing in $\gamma$, tends to $0$ as $\gamma\to0$ and to the audit trail's gain $V_A=s-\max\{0,m\}$ as $\gamma\to\infty$, and $V_C<V_A$ for every $\gamma$. With fixed costs $F_C\le F_A$ of class tags and of the audit trail, the audit trail is preferred to class tags exactly when $F_A-F_C<V_A-V_C(\gamma)$, that is, when $\gamma$ lies below a threshold.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

Three consequences follow. First, cheap relabeling restores observational equivalence in equilibrium: as $\gamma\to0$ the optimal tag policy converges to the uniform cut and class tags are worth nothing, so the identification problem of Section~\ref{n:cost_sec} reappears as an incentive problem. Second, the optimal targeted restriction is partial. When uniform retention is worthwhile ($m\ge0$), the supervisor leaves the harmful label partly open, with a label gap proportional to the relabeling cost and decreasing in the restricted traders' private gain; when it is not ($m<0$), it also restricts the benign label, because that label is the hiding channel. Third, an audit trail that links accounts to owners keeps its full gain at every $\gamma$, so its advantage over class tags is largest where relabeling is cheapest. Proposition~\ref{prop:choice} holds behaviour fixed across regimes; here it responds to them. The model is stylised: linear values, one cycle, enforced retentions, and a quadratic concealment cost as in the costly-state-falsification literature \citep{lackerweinberg1989,crockermorgan1998}. The proof is in Supplement Section~\ref{app:relabel}; its algebra is checked in Lean~4 and its closed forms by brute force.'''

SUPP = r'''\section{The disclosure game with relabeling}\label{app:relabel}

\paragraph{Proof of Proposition~\ref{n:relabel}.} (i) An $H$ trader's payoff is $b_H\{(1-z)y+zx\}-\gamma z^2/2=b_Hy+b_H(x-y)z-\gamma z^2/2$, strictly concave in $z$, so its maximizer on $[0,1]$ is the projection of $b_H(x-y)/\gamma$ (\nolinkurl{hide_best_response}).

(ii) Without disclosure, welfare is $mx$, maximized at $x=1$ if $m\ge0$ and $x=0$ otherwise. With an audit trail, $z=0$ and welfare $\chi yn_H+sx$ is maximized at $y=0$, $x=1$. Under class tags write $d=x-y\in[0,x]$ for the label gap. A policy with $b_Hd\ge\gamma$ makes $z^*=1$ and yields $mx-\chi\gamma/2$, less than the gap-free policy $y=x$; such policies are never optimal. For $b_Hd<\gamma$, substituting $z^*=b_Hd/\gamma$ gives
\[
W=mx+\chi\Big(ad-\frac{d^2}{2g}\Big)
\]
(\nolinkurl{tag_welfare_identity}). For fixed $x$ the bracket is concave in $d$, with maximum $a^2g/2$ at $\hat d=ag$ (\nolinkurl{label_gap_value}, \nolinkurl{label_gap_attained}). Since $\hat d=a\gamma/\{b_H(2a+b_H)\}<\gamma/b_H$, the restriction $b_Hd<\gamma$ never binds at the optimum, and the best gap is $\min\{x,\hat d\}$. The value of retention $x$ is therefore $\varphi(x)=sx-\chi x^2/(2g)$ for $x\le\hat d$ (using $m+\chi a=s$) and $\varphi(x)=mx+\chi a^2g/2$ for $x\ge\hat d$. The function $\varphi$ is concave and continuously differentiable, with $\varphi'(x)=s-\chi x/g$ below $\hat d$ and $\varphi'=m$ above it; the two derivatives agree at $\hat d$ because $s-\chi a=m$. If $m\ge0$, $\varphi$ is nondecreasing, so $x=1$ and the gap is $\min\{1,\hat d\}$, which gives the two values stated. If $m<0$, $\varphi'$ vanishes at $\hat x=sg/\chi$, and $\hat x<\hat d$ because $s<\chi a$; so $x=\min\{1,\hat x\}$, the gap is $x$, $y=0$, and the values follow. The equilibrium hiding share is $b_Hd/\gamma$ with $0<d\le\hat d$, so it is positive and at most $a/(2a+b_H)<1$ (\nolinkurl{hiding_share_lt_one}).

(iii) Each branch of $V_C$ is strictly increasing in $g$, which is proportional to $\gamma$: the interior branches are linear in $g$ with positive slope (\nolinkurl{tag_value_mono}) and the outer branches equal a constant minus $\chi/(2g)$ (\nolinkurl{outer_branch_mono}). Substitution shows that the branches agree where they meet ($ag=1$, respectively $sg=\chi$). As $g\to0$ the interior branches vanish; as $g\to\infty$ the outer branches tend to $\chi a=s-m$ (when $m\ge0$) and to $s$ (when $m<0$), which is $V_A$ in both cases. Each branch lies strictly below its limit (\nolinkurl{tags_below_audit_mnonneg}, \nolinkurl{tags_below_audit_mneg}, and directly for the outer branches). The regime comparison subtracts the fixed costs from these gains. Because $V_A-V_C(\gamma)$ falls continuously and strictly from $V_A$ to $0$, the set of $\gamma$ at which it exceeds $F_A-F_C$ is an interval $(0,\gamma^*)$, empty when $F_A-F_C\ge V_A$. $\square$

\paragraph{Checks.} The Lean~4 file \nolinkurl{PublicationRevision3.lean} checks the steps named above with only Lean's three standard axioms. The case analysis that assembles them is checked by \nolinkurl{t6/t6_check.py}: on 400 random economies, maximizing welfare over a grid of $(x,y)$ never exceeds the closed form, the closed-form policy attains it, and on three economies $V_C$ rises strictly over $\gamma\in[10^{-4},10^4]$ from near zero to within $0.1\%$ of $V_A$.

\paragraph{Scope.} The proposition takes the classes' net values as given and lets only the restricted class relabel; we restrict attention to policies with $y\le x$, under which benign traders have no reason to adopt the harmful label. A uniform cut is implementable from prices (Proposition~\ref{n:target}); we take it to be the only restriction available without class identity. Relabeling here is account routing, as in Proposition~\ref{prop:strategic}(i); an audit trail that links sub-accounts to owners removes it. If instead classes are defined by measured behaviour, hiding requires changing behaviour, which changes harm as well, and the model would need a joint cost of disguise and harm. Repeated cycles, learning about $\chi$ and enforcement costs are outside the model.'''

